$$\\begin{cases} x + y = 10 \\\\ x - y = 4 \\end{cases}$$', NULL, $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"$3$"},{"key":"B","text":"$6$"},{"key":"C","text":"$7$"},{"key":"D","text":"$14$"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'C', NULL, E'Adding the two equations eliminates $y$: $(x + y) + (x - y) = 10 + 4$, which gives $2x = 14$, so $x = 7$. The distractor $3$ is the value of $y$ (since $y = 10 - 7 = 3$), not $x$. The distractor $6$ results from an arithmetic error such as computing $\\frac{14}{2}$ incorrectly. The distractor $14$ is the intermediate value $2x$ before dividing by $2$.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"misread_question"},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"arithmetic_slip"},"C":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 60, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-17"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-17","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);



























































- Monarch butterflies migrate up to 3,000 miles each year between Canada and central Mexico.
- Milkweed is the only plant on which monarch caterpillars feed.
- Between 1996 and 2020, the area occupied by monarchs at their Mexican wintering grounds declined by approximately 80%.
- Widespread herbicide use in agricultural regions of the United States Midwest has eliminated an estimated 1.3 billion milkweed stems since the late 1990s.
- Conservation groups have launched campaigns encouraging homeowners to plant milkweed in gardens and along roadsides.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Monarch butterflies undertake one of nature's most impressive journeys, traveling up to 3,000 miles between Canada and central Mexico each year."},{"key":"B","text":"The dramatic decline in monarch butterfly populations is largely driven by the loss of 1.3 billion milkweed stems — the sole food source for monarch caterpillars — due to widespread herbicide use in the Midwest."},{"key":"C","text":"Conservation organizations have responded to declining monarch populations by encouraging homeowners to plant milkweed in their gardens and along roadsides."},{"key":"D","text":"Since 1996, the area occupied by monarchs at their Mexican wintering grounds has decreased by about 80%, prompting concern among scientists and conservationists."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'The correct choice highlights the primary threat by connecting the cause (herbicide-driven milkweed loss) to its consequence (population decline), drawing on the notes about milkweed being the sole caterpillar food source and the 1.3 billion stems lost. The choice about the 3,000-mile migration describes an impressive behavior but does not address any threat. The choice about conservation campaigns focuses on the response to the problem rather than identifying the threat itself. The choice citing the 80% decline at wintering grounds documents the extent of the population decrease but does not identify what is causing it.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 75, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-17"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-17","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);








Nutritionist Laura Henning recommends that athletes consume protein within thirty minutes of completing a workout. Henning argues that this narrow post-exercise window is critical because muscles are most receptive to amino acids immediately after exertion, and delaying protein intake significantly reduces the body''s ability to repair and build muscle tissue.

Text 2:
A 2022 meta-analysis led by sports scientist Marco Delgado examined forty-three studies on post-exercise protein timing and found no significant difference in muscle growth between athletes who consumed protein immediately after exercise and those who consumed it up to four hours later. Delgado concluded that total daily protein intake matters far more than the precise timing of consumption relative to a workout.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"By agreeing that consuming protein within thirty minutes is essential for muscle repair."},{"key":"B","text":"By arguing that protein consumption has no effect on muscle growth regardless of timing."},{"key":"C","text":"By presenting evidence that the strict timing window Henning describes is not supported by research."},{"key":"D","text":"By suggesting that Henning's recommendation applies only to professional athletes, not amateurs."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'C', NULL, E'The correct answer is the choice stating that Delgado''s research shows the strict timing window is not supported by research. Text 2 directly reports that Delgado''s meta-analysis found no significant difference between immediate and delayed protein consumption, undermining Henning''s claim about a critical thirty-minute window. The choice agreeing that thirty-minute timing is essential is wrong because Delgado''s findings explicitly contradict this claim. The choice stating protein has no effect on muscle growth is wrong because Delgado emphasizes that total daily protein intake does matter; he disputes only the narrow timing requirement. The choice limiting Henning''s claim to professionals is wrong because Delgado''s study does not distinguish between professional and amateur athletes; it broadly challenges the timing hypothesis across all populations studied.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"C":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 60, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-17"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-17","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);


Archaeologist Elena Vasquez argues that the construction of monumental stone architecture in ancient Mesopotamia was organized through centralized state authority. Vasquez points to administrative clay tablets found at temple complexes that record detailed labor quotas, material inventories, and wage distributions, all bearing official seals. She concludes that only a hierarchical bureaucracy could have coordinated the thousands of workers, quarried stone shipments, and specialized artisans required for structures like the ziggurats of Ur.

Text 2:
Archaeologist Kwame Asante, while acknowledging the administrative tablets Vasquez cites, interprets them differently. Asante argues that the tablets document coordination rather than coercion — they reflect agreements among semi-autonomous communities that voluntarily pooled labor for collective religious purposes. He points to variations in tablet formatting across different temple sites as evidence that record-keeping practices were locally developed rather than imposed from a central authority. Asante contends that monumental construction was a product of distributed cooperation among communities with shared religious motivations, not top-down state direction.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Whether the administrative tablets found at temple complexes are authentic historical documents."},{"key":"B","text":"Whether the clay tablets reflect centralized state control over construction or voluntary coordination among independent communities."},{"key":"C","text":"Whether the ziggurats of Ur required large numbers of workers and specialized artisans to construct."},{"key":"D","text":"Whether religious motivations played any role in the construction of monumental architecture."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'The correct answer is the choice about whether the tablets reflect centralized state control or voluntary coordination among independent communities. Vasquez interprets the tablets as evidence of hierarchical bureaucracy directing labor, while Asante reads the same tablets as documenting agreements among semi-autonomous communities that cooperated voluntarily — the core disagreement is over what the tablets reveal about the organizational structure behind construction. The choice about tablet authenticity is wrong because Asante explicitly acknowledges the tablets Vasquez cites; neither author questions whether the documents are genuine. The choice about requiring large numbers of workers is wrong because Asante does not dispute the scale of labor involved; he disputes how that labor was organized, not whether it was needed. The choice about whether religion played any role is wrong because both authors acknowledge religious purposes — Vasquez connects the tablets to temple complexes, and Asante explicitly describes shared religious motivations — so religion''s involvement is not contested between them.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-17"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-17","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);




Urban planner Kenji Murakami advocates for the widespread construction of dedicated bicycle lanes as the most effective strategy for reducing urban traffic congestion. Murakami points to cities like Copenhagen and Amsterdam, where extensive cycling infrastructure has led to a dramatic shift from car travel to bicycle commuting, resulting in shorter average commute times and lower carbon emissions.

Text 2:
Transportation researcher Sonia Agarwal contends that while bicycle infrastructure benefits cities with mild climates and flat terrain, it cannot serve as a universal congestion solution. Agarwal notes that cities with extreme heat, heavy rainfall, steep hills, or sprawling low-density layouts face fundamental barriers to mass bicycle adoption. She argues that investment in high-frequency public transit networks yields greater congestion relief across a wider range of urban geographies.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Text 2 argues that the strategy endorsed in Text 1 has significant limitations and proposes an alternative approach with broader applicability."},{"key":"B","text":"Text 2 provides additional evidence supporting the effectiveness of the strategy described in Text 1."},{"key":"C","text":"Text 2 disputes the claim in Text 1 that Copenhagen and Amsterdam have successfully reduced traffic congestion."},{"key":"D","text":"Text 2 addresses the environmental impact of urban transportation, a topic not discussed in Text 1."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'The correct answer is the choice stating Text 2 argues the strategy in Text 1 has limitations and proposes an alternative with broader applicability. Agarwal acknowledges that bicycle infrastructure works in some contexts but identifies geographic and climatic barriers and recommends high-frequency public transit as a more universally effective solution. The choice claiming Text 2 supports Text 1''s strategy is wrong because Agarwal challenges the generalizability of bicycle infrastructure rather than reinforcing its value. The choice about disputing Copenhagen and Amsterdam''s success is wrong because Agarwal does not question those cities'' outcomes; she argues that their favorable conditions cannot be assumed elsewhere. The choice about environmental impact being a new topic is wrong because Text 1 already mentions lower carbon emissions, and Text 2''s focus is on congestion relief across geographies, not on introducing environmental concerns.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 75, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-17"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-17","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);


- France attempted to build a canal across Panama beginning in 1881 but abandoned the project in 1889 after approximately 22,000 workers died, primarily from yellow fever and malaria.
- The United States took over the project in 1904 under the direction of chief engineer John Frank Stevens.
- Dr. William Gorgas, the chief sanitary officer, implemented mosquito-control measures including draining standing water, screening buildings, and fumigating worker housing.
- After Gorgas''s sanitation campaign reduced disease rates by over 90%, large-scale excavation of the canal proceeded on schedule.
- The canal opened to traffic in August 1914.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"The Panama Canal, which opened in August 1914, was originally attempted by France in 1881 before the United States took over the project in 1904 under chief engineer John Frank Stevens."},{"key":"B","text":"After France's failed attempt that cost 22,000 lives, the United States succeeded in building the Panama Canal only after Dr. William Gorgas's mosquito-control campaign reduced disease rates by over 90%, enabling large-scale excavation to proceed."},{"key":"C","text":"Dr. William Gorgas implemented several mosquito-control measures during the construction of the Panama Canal, including draining standing water, screening buildings, and fumigating worker housing."},{"key":"D","text":"The construction of the Panama Canal was a remarkable engineering achievement, requiring the excavation of millions of cubic yards of earth and the design of a lock system to raise ships across the continental divide."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'The correct choice directly supports the argument that the canal required solving a public health crisis by linking France''s catastrophic death toll from disease to the success that followed Dr. Gorgas''s sanitation campaign, showing that construction could not proceed until the health crisis was resolved. The choice about the canal''s timeline and leadership provides historical context but does not connect public health to construction success. The choice listing Gorgas''s specific mosquito-control methods describes the sanitation measures but does not make the argument that these measures were necessary for the project to succeed. The choice about engineering achievement discusses excavation and lock design, which are not supported by the notes and shift focus to engineering rather than the public health argument.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"evidence_mismatch"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-17"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-17","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);




- Tardigrades (water bears) are microscopic animals known for surviving extreme conditions, including vacuum, radiation, and temperatures near absolute zero.
- Scientists have generally assumed that tardigrades endure these conditions by entering a desiccated state called a tun, in which all metabolic activity ceases.
- In 2024, Dr. Kenji Yamamoto''s laboratory reported that a population of tardigrades exposed to near-absolute-zero temperatures showed faint but measurable metabolic signatures, suggesting they remained minimally active rather than fully shutting down.
- The finding was based on calorimetry readings from a single experimental trial with 200 specimens.
- No other laboratory has yet attempted to reproduce Dr. Yamamoto''s results.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Tardigrades are remarkable organisms capable of surviving vacuum, radiation, and temperatures near absolute zero, making them one of the most resilient life forms known to science."},{"key":"B","text":"Dr. Kenji Yamamoto's 2024 finding that tardigrades may remain metabolically active at near-absolute-zero temperatures challenges the long-held assumption that they survive by fully ceasing metabolic activity, though the result — drawn from a single trial — awaits independent replication."},{"key":"C","text":"Although no other laboratory has replicated the finding, Dr. Yamamoto used calorimetry readings from 200 specimens to detect metabolic signatures in tardigrades exposed to extreme cold."},{"key":"D","text":"Scientists have long believed that tardigrades survive extreme conditions by entering a tun state in which all metabolic activity ceases, a view supported by decades of observational research."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'The correct choice accomplishes both parts of the rhetorical goal: it identifies the challenged assumption (that tardigrades survive by ceasing all metabolic activity), presents the contradicting discovery (faint metabolic signatures at near-absolute-zero), and explicitly notes the limitation that the result comes from a single trial and has not been independently replicated. The choice describing tardigrades'' resilience provides general background about the organisms without addressing either the challenged assumption or the replication concern. The choice mentioning calorimetry and 200 specimens notes the lack of replication and describes the methodology but does not articulate which assumption is being challenged or why the finding matters. The choice about the tun state and decades of research presents only the traditional view without introducing the new discovery or the replication caveat.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-17"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-17","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);








